cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, corpus, or its results, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "hypocontra"
version: "2.0.0"
date-released: "2026-09-11"
abstract: >
  HypoContra: a 103-pair curated corpus (rounds 1-17) and evaluation
  pipeline for detecting conceptual contradictions between scientific
  hypotheses in NLP/CS literature, plus three trained and evaluated
  baseline NLI models. A curated candidate-selection strategy (mining
  explicit contrast markers from the full text of NLP survey papers, not
  automatic topical clustering) yielded 16/17 (94%) genuine contradictions
  in the pilot versus 1/97 (1%) for clustering, and scaled to 103 curated
  pairs with Cohen's kappa = 0.556 (five-way) / 0.607 (three-way, n=85).
  Three baseline NLI models were trained and evaluated on this corpus
  (rule-based macro F1=0.310, fine-tuned RoBERTa=0.575, LLM few-shot=0.690,
  the latter with disclosed evaluation circularity), plus a cross-domain
  generalization check (fine-tuned on the Alamri & Stevenson 2016
  biomedical corpus, zero-shot evaluated on HypoContra, F1=0.378). Labels
  are assigned by two independent AI annotation passes (Claude and a
  locally-hosted Gemma model, not human annotators) -- disclosed as a
  materially weaker validity claim than the original methodology's
  human-human inter-annotator agreement protocol, and a permanent
  characteristic of this corpus (replacing it with human annotation was
  deliberately decided against, not deferred).
